WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.200
Could you imagine Dumb and Dumber&nbsp;
with a couple of Amigos? How about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.680
Ghost Rider and Dracula? Could&nbsp;
a certain SNL legend have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.117
The Cable Guy? These are the what-if&nbsp;
casting tales of Jim Carrey movies.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.220
It's hard to imagine anyone besides Jim Carrey&nbsp;
playing Miami pet detective Ace Ventura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:23.160
the role that shot him to box office stardom in&nbsp;
February 1994. However, at the time that producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.960
were casting the role, Carrey actually wasn't&nbsp;
the first one they approached. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.760
Rick Moranis of Honey, I Shrunk the Kids was&nbsp;
offered the part, but he ultimately passed.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.860
Moranis had established a successful acting career&nbsp;
during the '80s, in the likes of Ghostbusters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.260
Spaceballs, and Little Shop of Horrors. But by&nbsp;
the time he was courted to play Ace Ventura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.600
he was intentionally taking fewer roles after&nbsp;
his wife Ann Belsky died of breast cancer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.300
February 1991. By 1997, he'd essentially&nbsp;
retired from acting to raise his two kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.020
although he occasionally did voiceover&nbsp;
work and other creative projects.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.220
Moranis ultimately never regretted&nbsp;
taking this hiatus. In 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.580
he told The Hollywood Reporter that&nbsp;
he looked back fondly at working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.600
the interesting people he met during the&nbsp;
height of his showbiz career. As he put it,

00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:13.886
"I have absolutely no regrets&nbsp;
whatsoever. My life is wonderful."

00:01:14.124 --> 00:01:19.200
After Rick Moranis passed on Ace Ventura,&nbsp;
producers looked to Jim Carrey's In Living Color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.540
castmate David Allen Grier, which Grier revealed&nbsp;
during a 2019 appearance on The Howard Stern Show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.280
He also noted that they considered Rob Schneider,&nbsp;
who was a cast member on Saturday Night Live at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.440
the time. After reading the script, though, Grier&nbsp;
didn't see a box office hit, so he passed. Besides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.080
Moranis and Grier, the producers also considered&nbsp;
Whoopi Goldberg, Judd Nelson, and Alan Rickman.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.460
Once Carrey got involved, he helped shape the&nbsp;
character and script alongside writer-director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.020
Tom Shadyac. As Carrey explained&nbsp;
to the Los Angeles Times in 1994,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.120
"From the beginning of my involvement, I said that&nbsp;
the character had to be rock 'n' roll. [...] I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.980
wanted to be unstoppably ridiculous, and they&nbsp;
let me go wild. [...] This movie was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.577
either be something that people really went&nbsp;
for, or it was going to ruin me completely."

00:02:03.716 --> 00:02:06.222
"Alrighty then!"

00:02:06.222 --> 00:02:10.680
Lauren Holly began her acting career on TV&nbsp;
in the '80s on a couple episodes of the cop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.840
drama Hill Street Blues before making her way&nbsp;
to the soap opera All My Children. Jim Carrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.160
lobbied for her to play the part of Melissa in&nbsp;
Ace Ventura, but she turned that down because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.480
of scheduling conflicts with her ongoing&nbsp;
role on the show Picket Fences. Melissa was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.000
ultimately played by Courteney Cox instead. Then&nbsp;
in 1995, Holly admitted to Entertainment Weekly,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.040
"I kicked myself when it came out."

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.000
Holly eventually got another chance to&nbsp;
co-star with Carrey later that year in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.040
Dumb and Dumber, and she said of that movie,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.080
"The minute I heard it was Jim and Jeff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.800
I definitely wanted to be a part of&nbsp;
it… I think my hardest job is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.700
to keep a straight face and not ruin any&nbsp;
of the takes — believe me, that's tough."

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.880
Holly and Carrey fell in love while making Dumb&nbsp;
and Dumber and got married in 1996, after Carrey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.308
divorce from his first marriage was finalized.&nbsp;
Alas, their union lasted less than a year.

00:02:58.756 --> 00:03:03.420
Jim Carrey, Jeff Daniels, and Lauren Holly&nbsp;
were the perfect stars for Dumb and Dumber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.980
but they weren't the lineup that the studio&nbsp;
originally envisioned. As recounted by E! News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.940
New Line Cinema felt that they were taking a&nbsp;
chance in letting the novice Farrelly brothers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:16.380
direct their own screenplay, so they wanted&nbsp;
to be closely involved in the casting process.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.420
Martin Short was reportedly New Line's first&nbsp;
pick for Lloyd, but he ultimately turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.340
the part. He hasn't spoken publicly about that&nbsp;
decision, but Vulture speculated that he may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.240
been apprehensive about starring in the debut&nbsp;
comedy of two directors with no track record.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.080
Bobby Farrelly later told The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter that they were trying to cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.960
the movie for a couple of years,&nbsp;
but they kept getting turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.460
by everyone. Peter Farrelly added in that&nbsp;
same interview that in reality many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.600
actors weren't actually being informed of&nbsp;
the offers by their agents. As he put it,

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.160
"[It's] so hard to tell how many&nbsp;
actually passed, but we were told 100."

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.355
"So you're telling me there's a chance?"

00:03:54.045 --> 00:03:57.960
Martin Short wasn't the only big-time&nbsp;
comedian to turn down the Farrelly brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.100
or even the only big-time comedian with&nbsp;
"Martin '' in his name, as his longtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.000
collaborator and friend Steve Martin also&nbsp;
declined the role of Dumb and Dumber's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.800
Lloyd. His work in films like The Jerk and&nbsp;
Three Amigos! made him an obvious choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.540
but he was seriously in demand at the time,&nbsp;
so the competition to cast him was fierce.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.560
Eventually, though, one of the producers&nbsp;
got a script over to Jim Carrey, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.340
responded favorably. As Peter Farrelly&nbsp;
recalled to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.360
"When we met with him he was like,&nbsp;
he got it exactly like we got it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.880
and so we thought this guy&nbsp;
would be perfect for it."

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.480
When Carrey signed on, Ace Ventura:&nbsp;
Pet Detective hadn't come out yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.800
so contract negotiations became an issue&nbsp;
when their star re-negotiated his salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.840
after scoring big at the box office earlier in&nbsp;
the year. After locking down Carrey as Lloyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.400
the focus was then shifted to figuring out who&nbsp;
would play Harry. New Line reportedly wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:52.800
initially interested in casting Jeff Daniels. As&nbsp;
Bobby Farrelly noted to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.100
"Daniels was not the obvious&nbsp;
choice because he hadn't done any,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.320
you know, out-and-out comedies before that."

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.920
After they read together, Carrey&nbsp;
lobbied for Daniels to get the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.978
and the latter accepted the offer, against his&nbsp;
agent's advice and at a discounted pay rate.

00:05:07.122 --> 00:05:12.120
Before Carrey and Daniels, and before everyone&nbsp;
else they considered, the Farrellys reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.380
originally envisioned Nicolas Cage and Gary&nbsp;
Oldman as Lloyd and Harry in Dumb and Dumber.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:21.240
Cage confirmed this in 2012, when he told the&nbsp;
Huffington Post that he was indeed in talks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:27.540
Carrey, whom he starred alongside in 1986's Peggy&nbsp;
Sue Got Married. But Cage ultimately turned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.980
role down so that he could instead star in Leaving&nbsp;
Las Vegas, for which he ended up winning an Oscar.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.240
Years later, Cage had another opportunity to&nbsp;
book a part that eventually went to Carrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.160
again. In 2017, director Michel Gondry&nbsp;
told The Daily Beast that he approached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.080
Cage to play Joel in 2004's Eternal&nbsp;
Sunshine of the Spotless Mind before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.700
ultimately choosing to cast Carrey against&nbsp;
type. After Cage became an Oscar winner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.440
the competition to cast him in an independent&nbsp;
film was quite stiff. Gondry also told Vanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.880
Fair that watching Carrey on the set of&nbsp;
2003's Bruce Almighty convinced him that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.020
he could handle the role, because he saw&nbsp;
something lonely behind his comedic facade.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.575
"That's high praise."

00:06:06.706 --> 00:06:12.000
Robin Williams was all set to play the Joker&nbsp;
in 1989's Batman, but he ultimately lost the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.200
part to Jack Nicholson, who went on to make&nbsp;
quite the impression as the Clown Prince of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.160
Crime. Williams felt that Warner Bros.&nbsp;
had used him as bait to lure Nicholson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.180
and he was disappointed to lose out on a role&nbsp;
he believed he would have been perfect for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.440
Despite that setback, he maintained hopes&nbsp;
of eventually playing a Batman villain.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.080
In a 2015 interview with The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.980
Batman Forever screenwriter Lee Batchler&nbsp;
revealed that they actually wrote the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.540
Riddler with Williams' voice in mind.&nbsp;
And back in 1993, Entertainment Weekly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:43.800
reported that Williams was excited to read&nbsp;
the script. But he eventually turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.960
the role without offering any public comment&nbsp;
about why he declined. As an insider told EW,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.740
"He believed the character was too&nbsp;
intellectual and not as comedic as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.320
the Riddler played by Frank&nbsp;
Gorshin on the TV series."

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.740
Others suggested that Williams&nbsp;
was wary of Warner Bros. after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.980
what happened with the Joker. Either&nbsp;
way, after he turned down the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:06.060
Carrey was eventually cast as The Riddler. But&nbsp;
Williams was still interested in playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.380
character in Christopher Nolan's version&nbsp;
of the franchise, telling Empire in 2012,

00:07:10.742 --> 00:07:12.658
"I'd play The Riddler in the next Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.658 --> 00:07:16.967
although it'd be hard to top Heath as the&nbsp;
villain. And I'm a little hairy for tights."

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.840
Believe it or not, Chris Farley was the&nbsp;
first actor attached to 1996's The Cable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.360
Guy before Jim Carrey came into the picture.&nbsp;
As reported by Variety, after Columbia Pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.560
bought the script, they cast Farley to&nbsp;
star in the title role. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.160
though, Paramount Pictures still had the&nbsp;
comic actor signed to a two-movie deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:40.320
and he had to film Black Sheep to fulfill that&nbsp;
contract. Farley and Columbia agreed that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.580
could film The Cable Guy after finishing out his&nbsp;
stint as a cast member on Saturday Night Live.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.112
"Oh! Son of a, that's gonna leave a mark."

00:07:49.112 --> 00:07:52.080
But Farley ultimately dropped&nbsp;
out of the project because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.040
reportedly didn't want to be locked into&nbsp;
anything so far in the future. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:01.800
both his career and life were cut short when&nbsp;
he died at the age of 33 in December 1997.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.060
Carrey's turn in The Cable Guy wound up being&nbsp;
a performance that changed Hollywood forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.880
He was on a career high coming off his multiple&nbsp;
hits in 1994, and he negotiated a $20 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.980
payday for the dark comedy. As reported by&nbsp;
Variety, he was the first actor to command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:23.040
a salary of that size. Looking back in 2021,&nbsp;
Variety deals reporter Mike Fleming Jr. noted,

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.700
"Every major top-of-the-line movie&nbsp;
star got a $5 million raise."

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:30.960
At the time, Carrey's payday was bigger&nbsp;
news than The Cable Guy itself, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.380
failed to really click at the box office,&nbsp;
though it's since become a cult favorite.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.660
1998's The Truman Show predicted the&nbsp;
shift towards our reality TV culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.500
with a bold story about a man who doesn't&nbsp;
know that he's the star of a show about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.000
own life. The movie gave Jim Carrey a rare&nbsp;
opportunity to take on a more serious role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.998
which would lead to more dramatic&nbsp;
opportunities down the road.

00:08:50.998 --> 00:08:54.960
"Oh, and in case I don't see you, good&nbsp;
afternoon, good evening, and good night."

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.580
As reported by Variety, Carrey wanted the&nbsp;
opportunity to branch out and show his range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:04.620
so badly that he even took a pay cut, making&nbsp;
only $12 million instead of his typical $20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:09.300
million fee. Director Peter Weir was so&nbsp;
set on Carrey taking the lead role that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.260
he waited a year to film because the actor&nbsp;
was busy making Liar Liar and The Cable Guy.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.340
Although this might make it sound like Weir&nbsp;
knew exactly who he wanted in his cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:21.720
there was still a last-minute shake-up, as&nbsp;
Dennis Hopper was actually originally set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.740
to play the antagonist Christof. But,&nbsp;
as reported by the Los Angeles Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:30.420
after just one day of shooting, Hopper left&nbsp;
the production due to "creative differences"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.640
and was later replaced by Ed Harris. As Harris&nbsp;
recalled to The Hollywood Reporter in 2021,

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.200
"They were shooting a scene in Malibu,&nbsp;
and I live in Malibu. So I drove up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:45.000
coast to meet with Peter. We talked. It was&nbsp;
a Friday. I think I started work on Monday."

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.280
In April 2010, The Wrap reported that&nbsp;
Ben Stiller was talking to director Noah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.420
Baumbach about starring in the title role&nbsp;
of the adaptation of the children's book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:59.460
Mr. Popper's Penguins. Their interpretation of&nbsp;
the project was a little bizarre and included an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:03.420
entirely new storyline with football player&nbsp;
Peyton Manning and his real estate agent.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.140
Two months later, it was reported that&nbsp;
Stiller had walked away from the role when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.160
Baumbach ultimately passed on the project.&nbsp;
It seemed that a big part of the appeal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.940
the actor was a chance to work with his&nbsp;
frequent collaborator again. Afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:18.240
the likes of Jack Black and Owen Wilson&nbsp;
were on the short list of actors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.900
the studio was interested in casting, but&nbsp;
Jim Carrey was at the top of their list.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.560
Once Mark Waters came on board as&nbsp;
the director, he called up Carrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.680
in the hopes that he would star. They'd&nbsp;
been trying to work together for a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.480
and Carrey was the only person he could see in&nbsp;
the role. As Waters told Entertainment Weekly,

00:10:33.698 --> 00:10:36.518
"I got lucky. I asked for Jim and I got him."

00:10:36.518 --> 00:10:40.380
Although Waters was thrilled to work&nbsp;
with Carrey, the movie was ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.220
tepidly received by critics and didn't&nbsp;
do very well at the box office either.

